[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Troubleshooting Deadlocks, Thread Dumps, and Hung Pipelines in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins instances often scale to handle hundreds of concurrent builds, but resource contention, improper plugin locking, or blocked I/O can lead to frozen pipelines. This tutorial covers how to diagnose deadlocks, capture thread dumps, and resolve hung pipelines in a production Jenkins environment."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Identifying Hung Pipelines"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A hung pipeline typically manifests as a build that remains in an executing state indefinitely without logging new output. Before diving into thread dumps, confirm the pipeline is actually hung and not just waiting on a slow external resource like a Kubernetes pod or database migration."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Check the Pipeline Stage View to isolate the exact step where execution stopped."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inspect agent availability and ensure network connectivity between the Jenkins controller and the target agent node."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Capturing Thread Dumps on the Controller"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"When the Jenkins controller itself becomes unresponsive or multiple pipelines hang simultaneously, a thread dump is essential. It provides a snapshot of every active Java thread and its current call stack."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To capture a thread dump on a Linux-based controller, find the process ID (PID) of the Jenkins Java process and use the jstack utility."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"ps aux | grep jenkins
sudo jstack <JENKINS_PID> > /tmp/jenkins_thread_dump.txt"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alternatively, if you have administrative access to the Jenkins web UI, you can navigate to Manage Jenkins > System Information > Thread Dumps to view active threads directly in your browser."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Analyzing Thread Dumps for Deadlocks"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A deadlock occurs when two or more threads are blocked forever, waiting for each other to release locks. Modern thread dumps usually include an explicit deadlock analysis section at the bottom."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Search the dump file for the keyword "deadlock" or "Found one Java-level deadlock"."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identify the thread IDs involved and trace their stack traces to determine which custom plugin or pipeline step caused the contention."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Troubleshooting Agent and Pipeline Hangs via Script Console"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a specific pipeline is frozen and cannot be aborted through the standard UI, administrators can use the Jenkins Script Console (Manage Jenkins > Script Console) to forcibly terminate the offending thread or build."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Groovy script to inspect running builds and interrupt them
def jobName = "YourPipelineJobName"
def buildNumber = 123
def run = Jenkins.instance.getItem(jobName).getBuildByNumber(buildNumber)
if (run.isBuilding()) {
    run.executor.interrupt()
    println("Interrupted build " + buildNumber)
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Prevention and Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always set explicit timeouts on pipeline steps (using the timeout block) to prevent tasks from hanging indefinitely."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep Jenkins and all installed plugins updated to avoid known deadlock bugs in older plugin versions."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor JVM heap usage and garbage collection logs to ensure memory exhaustion is not contributing to thread freezing."}]
